WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.400
Lifting a cup and lifting a suitcase can use the same muscle.

2
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.680
Who decides how hard it pulls?

3
00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:13.713
The decision ends in the spinal cord, at cells called alpha motor neurons.

4
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:18.033
Each motor neuron's axon branches to many muscle fibres.

5
00:00:18.993 --> 00:00:21.433
The neuron and its fibres make one motor unit.

6
00:00:21.983 --> 00:00:25.163
One spike normally makes every fibre of its unit twitch.

7
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:29.917
All the motor neurons that supply one muscle make up its motor pool.

8
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:34.590
Commands come from the brain, stretch sensors, pain and rhythm circuits.

9
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:37.163
All of them end on the motor neurons.

10
00:00:38.203 --> 00:00:40.183
Sherrington called them the final common path.

11
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:44.893
Picture one motor neuron's voltage as a bar, with a line at threshold.

12
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:48.247
Excitation pushes the bar up, toward the line.

13
00:00:49.187 --> 00:00:50.347
Inhibition holds it back.

14
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:52.707
Usually it pulls the bar down.

15
00:00:53.527 --> 00:00:55.827
Its open channels also blunt excitation.

16
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.720
When the net current lifts the bar to the line, the cell fires.

17
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:02.953
So ask two questions of any spinal circuit.

18
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:06.133
Which motor pools does it push up, and which down?

19
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.573
And within a pool, which cells reach threshold first?

20
00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:14.743
Take a pool of small cells, which make weak units, and large cells, which make

21
00:01:14.743 --> 00:01:15.743
strong units.

22
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:19.137
The input to the whole pool rises a little at a time.

23
00:01:19.897 --> 00:01:20.977
Which cells fire first?

24
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:31.600
The small ones. Many people guess the large ones, because big seems strong.

25
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.500
Firing depends on how far the input moves the voltage.

26
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.560
The same input moves a small cell further.

27
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:43.213
A small cell has less membrane, so fewer ways for current to leave.

28
00:01:44.113 --> 00:01:45.593
Its input resistance is higher.

29
00:01:46.217 --> 00:01:48.117
Think of a bathtub with fewer drains.

30
00:01:49.077 --> 00:01:51.157
The same inflow raises the water higher.

31
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:54.217
On screen is Ohm's law for a motor neuron.

32
00:01:55.217 --> 00:01:57.817
Higher input resistance means less current is needed.

33
00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:01.517
That order, small before large, is the size principle.

34
00:02:02.557 --> 00:02:05.437
Under shared input it is a strong tendency, not a law.

35
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:11.470
There is a catch. A large cell has room for more synapses, so it can collect more

36
00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:12.433
input.

37
00:02:12.433 --> 00:02:17.113
In this simple model, small cells still go first if effective current grows less

38
00:02:17.113 --> 00:02:20.273
steeply with size than input resistance falls.

39
00:02:21.293 --> 00:02:23.853
Real current distributions depend on the pathway.

40
00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:27.977
So, with equal thresholds, the small cells still go first.

41
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.840
A pool has a second way to add force.

42
00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.540
Active units fire faster, so their twitches add up.

43
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.970
Every command ends as current on motor neurons.

44
00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.050
Which pools go up or down, and who fires first?
